erefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x-(2y-2x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534484 17592186045417 false] [17592186415861 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/92/92b9a6c83dd18a5599bbee71d714b26fddd62eb32917a5df983edc232664067b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534484 17592186045417 true] [17592186415861 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}